# Target C-II — optimized adjacent backbone reduces the switching caterpillar forest to at most `542` edges

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only source-level structural strengthening  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** audited `0.7404D` smallest-leaf weighted center fan; corrected complement-dual path construction; global strict-rhombus angular-defect budget; matched atomic/Section-171 diameter-edge density bound; adjacent Pareto and switching-caterpillar refinements  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_adjacent_optimized_path_543.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The original adjacent-cut theorem chose

\[
\lambda=0.677,
\qquad r=0.488
\]

to keep the three averaged constants balanced.  That is not the right objective once
the remaining obstruction has been reduced to a finite switching diameter graph.
For blocker classification the useful quantity is the length of the selected good-leaf
backbone, because a longer path decreases the guaranteed number of switch rows on one
boundary.

Re-optimizing only this step, while keeping the same audited fan inputs and all later
interval/KKT arguments unchanged, nearly doubles the backbone density.  Combined with
the improved full diameter-edge count, the finite cusp shrinks from the original
`<21200` rows to at most `542` switching rows.

## Theorem OP543

Every sufficiently large survivor in the stated `t<=1.844` branch has consecutive
smallest-leaf O--O cuts `tau_i,tau_{i+1}` for which

\[
\boxed{N_i\le542,}
\tag{OP.1}

while simultaneously

\[
B_i=O(D^{-1}),
\qquad
 d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})=O(D^{-1}).
\tag{OP.2}

Moreover the switching diameter graph is the layer cut

\[
\delta_H(S_{i+1}\setminus S_i)
\]

and hence is a caterpillar forest.  Thus the remaining large-first-order active cusp
contains at most `542` diameter edges.

---

## 1. Re-optimize the good-leaf path rather than the balanced constant

Use exactly the parent fan inputs

\[
{\sum_\tau w_\tau d_\tau\over\sum_\tau w_\tau}
>0.7404-o(1),
\]

\[
{1\over D}\sum_\tau w_\tau>0.0848-o(1),
\]

and

\[
N_C<1.063D+o(D).
\]

Take the rational good-leaf threshold

\[
\boxed{\lambda=0.6404.}
\tag{OP.3}
\]

Weighted thresholding gives distinct good-leaf density at least

\[
\begin{aligned}
g
&={ (0.7404-0.6404)(0.0848)\over1.063-0.6404}\\
&=0.0200662565\ldots .
\end{aligned}
\tag{OP.4}

Now choose the upper center block fraction `r` by requiring the lower-phase neighbor
reserve to be the fixed positive value

\[
\boxed{d_{low}=10^{-4}.}
\tag{OP.5}
\]

Thus

\[
r={0.6404-0.0001\over1.063}
={6403\over10630}
=0.6023518344\ldots .
\tag{OP.6}
\]

There is no requirement that `r<1/2`; the parent proof uses only the sizes of the top
block and its complement.  Every good leaf has at least

\[
\begin{aligned}
d_{top}
&=\lambda-(1-r)(1.063)\\
&=0.2177
\end{aligned}
\tag{OP.7}

neighbors in the top block.  Double counting therefore gives one center train crossing
a simple complement-dual path of more than

\[
\boxed{
pD-o(D),
\qquad
p={d_{top}g\over r(1.063)}
=0.00682246453482\ldots .
}
\tag{OP.8}

This is about `1.914` times the old path-density constant
`0.00356417991995...`.

The lower-phase reserve is deliberately small but fixed.  Consequently the same global
angular-defect argument still gives

\[
\sum_jq_j
\le {C_{phase}\over10^{-4}}+o(1),
\tag{OP.9}
\]

which is a larger constant but remains `O(1)` and therefore preserves inverse-`D`
phase averaging on a linear path.

---

## 2. Use the current diameter-edge density, not `pi D`

The matched atomic/Section-171 ledger plus the uniform retained-mass theorem gives, as
proved in `target_c_adjacent_switch_count_1039_2026-09-15.md`,

\[
{h_H\over D}<1.851259926+o(1),
\tag{OP.10}
\]

where `h_H` is the total number of actual diameter edges.

Each interval row starts and stops at most twice along the selected path, so

\[
\boxed{
\sum_iN_i
<3.702519852D+o(D).
}
\tag{OP.11}

Divide by the optimized path density (OP.8):

\[
{3.702519852\over p}
=542.69536\ldots .
\tag{OP.12}

Thus the integer threshold `543` lies strictly above the count-only average.

---

## 3. Put all three properties on the same boundary

The switching KKT mass budget is unchanged:

\[
\sum_iDB_i\le2D.
\tag{OP.13}
\]

The optimized path has phase budget

\[
\sum_iD(q_i+q_{i+1})
\le {2C_{phase}\over10^{-4}}D+o(D).
\tag{OP.14}
\]

Use the general Pareto union bound with

\[
n=543,
\qquad a=b=10^{14}.
\tag{OP.15}
\]

The exact certificate gives bad-boundary densities

\[
0.00681863692688\ldots,
\]

\[
2\times10^{-14},
\]

and

\[
1.0633465\times10^{-9},
\]

whose sum is below

\[
p=0.00682246453482\ldots
\]

by more than

\[
3.8\times10^{-6}.
\]

Hence one boundary satisfies simultaneously

\[
N_i<543+o(1),
\qquad
DB_i<10^{14}+o(1),
\qquad
D(q_i+q_{i+1})<10^{14}+o(1).
\tag{OP.16}

For sufficiently large sources, integrality gives `N_i<=542`, while the latter two
bounds still imply precisely the asymptotic scales in (OP.2).

---

## 4. Updated finite-cusp normal form

Combine (OP.1)--(OP.2) with the adjacent switching-caterpillar theorem.  The exact
opposite two-cut multishear now has

```text
at most 542 large-first-order actual diameter rows
+ those rows form a bipartite caterpillar forest
+ they are exactly one vertex-layer cut of the full diameter thrackle
+ total switching KKT multiplier = O(1/D)
+ adjacent leaf directions differ by O(1/D)
+ all non-switching spanning rows cancel to o(1) at amplitude xi/D
```

The full-support KKT cusp still blocks a purely local old-contact descent.  The gain is
that the finite interface classification has become much smaller without changing the
geometric branch or introducing a new unproved input.

The rational choice above is close to count-optimal for this fan-threshold architecture:
letting `d_low` tend to zero numerically only moves the count average from
`542.695...` to about `542.53`.  Thus forcing at most `541` rows would require either a
stronger fan/path-density theorem or a smaller global diameter-edge budget, not further
rounding of these two thresholds.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only optimized-backbone strengthening of the adjacent
finite-cusp normal form. C-II and Level I remain open.
